You're my favorite all-time boy scout.

105
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:08,500
You're my favorite all-time boy scout.

106
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:11,500
He's my favorite all-time boy scout.

107
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:17,500
Write me a letter, send it by name

108
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:20,500
Email. that is folks.

109
00:06:20,500 --> 00:06:31,500
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

110
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:35,500
Brock Ford is the Scoutmaster Boy Scout Troop 53

111
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:37,500
in Maytown, Pennsylvania.

112
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:38,500
He wrote in to say

113
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:42,500
I discovered your podcast and blog about three months ago

114
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:44,500
after I was approached to become a Scoutmaster.

115
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:48,500
Last night was my first official meeting as Scoutmaster

116
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:50,500
and it was a train wreck.

117
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:54,500
The troop of 20 scouts are essentially acting as one big patrol

118
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:57,500
and they didn't have much planned for last night.

119
00:06:57,500 --> 00:07:00,500
To make things more difficult, a new scout just crossed over

120
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:04,500
and dad was displeased by the disorganization and horse play

121
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:06,500
at his son's first scout meeting.

122
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:09,500
We've discussed really implementing the patrol method

123
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:11,500
and our patrol leaders council has agreed.

124
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:15,500
Last night, the youth leaders worked on deciding how to reorganize patrols.

125
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:18,500
I realized that this is a long process

126
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:21,500
and that the scouts need lots of guidance,

127
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:24,500
since I don't think most of them would have any idea

128
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:26,500
of what it means to be a patrol leader,

129
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:28,500
I have a few questions for you.

130
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:29,500
We have a court of honor coming up

131
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:31,500
and we want the scouts to lead most of it,

132
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:33,500
so I was wondering if you have any ideas

133
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:35,500
or a template for a simple court of honor.

134
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:38,500
Traditionally, we've saved all the rank and merit badge patches

135
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:41,500
until the court of honor, but when I asked about this,

136
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:44,500
I sense everyone is agreeable to giving the award out

137
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:48,500
as soon as possible and recognizing it again at the court of honor.

138
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:50,500
Do you usually have youth give out all the patches

139
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:53,500
at the court of honor, or do scout masters usually give out things?

140
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:56,500
I also wanted to ask about how you handle

141
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:58,500
your scout master minute.

142
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:01,500
We haven't had one as a part of our troop meetings

143
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:04,500
and I'm wondering best how to get this started.

144
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:06,500
We usually do lots of announcements

145
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:08,500
at the end of the meeting

146
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:10,500
and I know the scouts get tired of this

147
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:12,500
because it takes a long time.

148
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:14,500
so the scout master would be humorous

149
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:16,500
or inspirational or mix it up

150
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:18,500
Well. Brock, first of all,

151
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:19,500
thanks for getting in touch

152
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:22,500
and congratulations on becoming a scout master.

153
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:24,500
I want you to zoom out a little bit

154
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:26,500
because, as a new scout master,

155
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:28,500
you're going to have a million details on your plate.

156
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:30,500
so instead of focusing on those details,

157
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:33,500
let's zoom out and look at the whole picture.

158
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:35,500
From that perspective

159
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:37,500
you'll begin to learn what's really important

160
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:39,500
and you'll get those things right

161
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:42,500
and many of the details will take care of themselves.

162
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:46,500
Disorganized, difficult kind of chaotic meetings

163
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:50,500
are nothing that I want you to aim at trying to fix,

164
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:53,500
no matter how many parents grumble about them,

165
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:56,500
and I don't want you to aim at fixing the meetings,

166
00:08:56,500 --> 00:08:58,500
because they're going to fix themselves

167
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:01,500
as you empower and train your youth leaders.

168
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:03,500
I've been doing this for 30 years now

169
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:05,500
and I've got to tell you most of our troop meetings

170
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:07,500
would be regarded as chaotic

171
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:10,500
and disorganized to any casual observer.

172
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:12,500
Learn to look

173
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:14,500
for promising moments and initiatives.

174
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:16,500
you'll see in your youth leaders,

175
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:18,500
and these will appear to be very small

176
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:20,500
and insignificant at first-

177
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:23,500
and build on these with a whole lot of praise and effort.

178
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:26,500
You're correct in saying that this is a long process,

179
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:29,500
and that's true, because it never stops.

180
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:31,500
It never arrives at completion.

181
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:33,500
You'll never reach a point where you have

182
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:36,500
totally arrived at anything resembling perfection.

183
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:39,500
The nature of our work as Scoutmasters

184
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:42,500
is building skills and competency in youth leadership.

185
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:45,500
As soon as they become competent in one area,

186
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:47,500
they move on to new challenges.

187
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:49,500
You'll always be developing,

188
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:52,500
but you'll never be finished.

189
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:54,500
It's not just a long process,

190
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:57,500
it is an infinitely long process.

191
00:09:57,500 --> 00:09:59,500
so your measured and responsive guidance

192
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:01,500
to the initiative you see

193
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:03,500
in your youth leaders is important,

194
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:06,500
but it's easy to go way too far.

195
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:08,500
A new patrol leader needs to learn

196
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:10,500
what to do in the next hour,

197
00:10:10,500 --> 00:10:13,500
not what he needs to do in the next year.

198
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:15,500
I will tell you from my experience:

199
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:17,500
adults tend to over instruct

200
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:19,500
and their guidance becomes interference.

201
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:21,500
if we're not cautious,

202
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:23,500
You won't ever have a cadre of

203
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:25,500
wholly competent, fully functioning patrol leaders.

204
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:27,500
but, handled the right way,

205
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:29,500
You'll have a lot of energized,

206
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:31,500
engaged Scouts learning

207
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:33,500
some very important things.

208
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:35,500
As for the Court of Honor,

209
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:37,500
it's not about getting things right or wrong.

210
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:40,500
it's about understanding the principles behind

211
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:42,500
why we have something called the Court of Honor.

212
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:44,500
If you haven't already

213
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:47,500
get a copy of the Guide to Advancement 2013

214
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:50,500
and read about Courts of Honor and Recognition,

215
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:52,500
The directions and answers there

216
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:54,500
are better than any I'm going to be able to give you

217
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:56,500
and then, taking those principles,

218
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:58,500
apply them to the Court of Honor

219
00:10:58,500 --> 00:10:59,500
you're about to have.

220
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:01,500
One of the things you'll learn

221
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:03,500
when you read the Guide to Advancement 2013

222
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:05,500
is that we want Scouts

223
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:07,500
to be recognized for their achievements

224
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:09,500
as soon as possible.

225
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:11,500
So, yes, we present them with the badge

226
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:12,500
as soon as we have it

227
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:14,500
and we hold on to the certificate

228
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:15,500
that comes with the badge

229
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:17,500
to present at the Court of Honor.

230
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:18,500
As for the Scout Master Minute,

231
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:20,500
that's the only adult

232
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:22,500
who has a speaking role

233
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:23,500
at troop meetings.

234
00:11:23,500 --> 00:11:25,500
And, yes, you can mix it up,

235
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:26,500
it can be humorous,

236
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:27,500
it can be inspiring,

237
00:11:27,500 --> 00:11:29,500
but it needs to be right there at a minute,

238
00:11:29,500 --> 00:11:31,500
and a minute is 60 seconds long.

239
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:34,500
so I find that I need to practice sometimes,

240
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:36,500
otherwise I'll just keep going

241
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:38,500
on and on and on. and Scouts,

242
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:41,500
they don't have time for that, do they?

243
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:44,500
I would tell the other adults in your troop:

244
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:46,500
if they have something to announce,

245
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:47,500
they can write it down

246
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:49,500
and you or the senior patrol leader

247
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:51,500
will take care of making the announcement

248
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:52,500
at the end of the meeting.

249
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:54,500
Not everybody is going to like this at first,

250
00:11:54,500 --> 00:11:55,500
but stand firm.

251
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:57,500
If you have this big, unfettered,

252
00:11:57,500 --> 00:11:59,500
open-ended announcements time

253
00:11:59,500 --> 00:12:01,500
at the end of a Scout Meeting,

254
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:03,500
people are going to find things to say

255
00:12:03,500 --> 00:12:05,500
and they're going to take a lot of time

256
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:06,500
saying that

257
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:07,500
If you go by the principle that

258
00:12:07,500 --> 00:12:09,500
the Scout Master has the only adult

259
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:11,500
speaking role at a troop meeting.

