00:19:36,500 --> 00:19:38,500
troop are willing to make this happen

489
00:19:38,500 --> 00:19:40,500
for him, he can go looking for adults

490
00:19:40,500 --> 00:19:42,500
that are helping scouts make this

491
00:19:42,500 --> 00:19:44,500
happen now. naturally he's loyal

492
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:46,500
to his troop and to his fellow

493
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:48,500
scouts, but that loyalty

494
00:19:48,500 --> 00:19:50,500
shouldn't restrict them from doing

495
00:19:50,500 --> 00:19:52,500
great things. Scouts who see

496
00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:54,500
this sort of spirit

497
00:19:54,500 --> 00:19:56,500
and this kind of engagement and

498
00:19:56,500 --> 00:19:58,500
excitement about scouting as

499
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:00,500
a threat to their program because,

500
00:20:00,500 --> 00:20:02,500
oh my gosh, the scout might

501
00:20:02,500 --> 00:20:04,500
plan something that conflicts with our

502
00:20:04,500 --> 00:20:06,500
plans- they're just plain wrong

503
00:20:06,500 --> 00:20:08,500
and they end up losing the

504
00:20:08,500 --> 00:20:10,500
loyalty and interest of scouts,

505
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:12,500
like your son, because of it.

506
00:20:12,500 --> 00:20:14,500
we do our best work as

507
00:20:14,500 --> 00:20:16,500
scouts when we find a way

508
00:20:16,500 --> 00:20:18,500
to make things happen for

509
00:20:18,500 --> 00:20:20,500
scouts rather than trying to

510
00:20:20,500 --> 00:20:22,500
control all the variables.

511
00:20:22,500 --> 00:20:24,500
Andrew West is an assistant

512
00:20:24,500 --> 00:20:26,500
scout master with troop 501

513
00:20:26,500 --> 00:20:28,500
in Cleveland, Ohio and he writes

514
00:20:28,500 --> 00:20:30,500
to ask this: what is the

